= The question, and the answer in brief <ch-question>

This guide answers one question:

#block(inset: (left: 14pt, y: 4pt), stroke: (left: 2pt + hair))[
  #text(size: 12pt, style: "italic")[Once artificial intelligence automates mental and physical labor entirely, what happens to our economy?]
]

It is an easy question to answer badly, and most answers go wrong in one of three ways.

The first is to reason from the past without knowing why the past turned out as it did. Machines have replaced human work for two hundred years and workers became richer, so they will again. That is a pattern, and patterns hold until the conditions behind them change. @ch-history finds the conditions.

The second is to confuse making things with sharing them out. People say that if nobody has a wage nobody can buy anything, so the economy will collapse, or automation will stop itself. Neither follows. An economy can produce in abundance and still leave most people with no claim on the product. @ch-distribution shows why these are separate matters.

The third is to confuse what machines can do with what they are being used to do. One camp looks at the capabilities of AI and announces that everything changes next year. The other looks at the unemployment rate and announces that nothing is happening. @ch-now shows that both are describing the same world.

== How the guide is built

The guide is meant to be checked as well as read. It is built as a chain, and the links are numbered.

- An *axiom* is a basic fact about how economies work, stated with the evidence for it. There are eight.
- A *definition* fixes the meaning of a term. There are five.
- A *hypothesis* is a claim about the world that the evidence supports and does not settle. There are six. Each is stated with my confidence in it and with what changes if it is wrong.
- A *proposition* is a conclusion. There are twenty-five. Each one names the earlier statements it rests on, so that a reader who doubts it can see exactly which link to test. A proposition that rests on a hypothesis is only as sure as the hypothesis, and says so.
- A *forecast* is a judgment about the future, with a probability attached. There are seven, all in Part IV.

Statements are referred to by letter and number: A3 is the third axiom, H2 the second hypothesis, P12 the twelfth proposition. In the electronic version each reference is a link. Chapters 2 to 14 each end with a box that says where the chain stands, and Appendix B lists the whole chain on two pages.

The chain of deduction runs from the first axiom to the twenty-fifth proposition, in Part III. Part IV is a different kind of writing. It is evidence and judgment, informed by the chain, and it is where I can most easily be wrong.

The guide has four parts. Part I sets out how a market economy distributes what it produces, and why earlier automation raised wages. Part II assumes that machines can do all work and works out what happens to wages, to production and to the distribution of income, saying at each step whether logic or evidence is carrying the weight. Part III asks who has the power to change that distribution. Part IV leaves the assumption behind, looks at the evidence as of November 2026, builds six scenarios for the next ten years and ends with my own best guess.

== The answer in brief

For readers who want the conclusion before the argument, here it is. Every sentence below is defended in the chapter named.

+ In a market economy, people receive a share of what is produced by owning something that production needs. Most people own little except their capacity to work, so wages are how most people get their share (@ch-economy).
+ Machines made workers richer for two centuries because every machine was narrow. There was always something left that only people could do, and whatever is scarce is well paid (@ch-history).
+ A machine that can do everything a person can do removes that protection. The wage for any work then cannot exceed what it costs to hire the machine, so it no longer rises in step with the economy. That much is logic. The evidence adds that where such machines are plentiful, hiring one costs less than keeping a person alive. This happened once before, to horses (@ch-wages).
+ The way there is a race. While any necessary task still needs people, wages rise if machines are built faster than tasks are handed over, and the people who can do what is left may prosper. When no such task remains, human work is worth the machine price whatever came before. "Nearly all" and "all" are different worlds (@ch-wages).
+ Production does not suffer. An economy with machine labor can produce far more than ours, and grow faster (@ch-growth).
+ Nor does it run out of customers. The income that used to be paid as wages is paid to owners, and production turns to serve them and to build more machines. The economy keeps running. It stops providing for most people (@ch-distribution).
+ Some paid work remains wherever a person is wanted as a person. It is what keeps labor's share of income, the part of all income that is paid for work, above zero. It reflects how income is distributed and cannot repair it (@ch-residual).
+ Majorities have had a share of output largely because they were needed as workers, soldiers and taxpayers. Automation removes that leverage over time, so a lasting claim on the output of machines is far easier to secure during the transition than after it (@ch-power).
+ Providing for everyone would cost a small fraction of what such an economy produces (@ch-power). Claims that are universal, automatic, visible and hard to reverse last longest; claims that must be voted again each year tend to shrink or lapse (@ch-policy).

That is the answer to the question as asked. Its short form is: *the economy does not break; the wage does, and the wage was doing more than anyone noticed.*

The forecast for the coming decade is more uncertain and is argued in Part IV. In short: machines will probably be able to do nearly all screen-based work around the end of this decade, and will take a further ten years or so to be put fully to that use. Robots will follow, in warehouses and factories within a few years and in general physical work roughly a decade behind. The change will most likely show up first as a slump in hiring, then as a recession from which office jobs do not return. By 2036 the United States will most likely be part of the way through: machines doing perhaps a quarter of all work, employment and labor's share of income each down by several points, policy a patchwork of dividends, public stakes and protected jobs, and the question of who owns the machine economy unsettled. I give that central scenario two chances in five, faster ones one in four, slower ones a little under one in four, and a well-managed one about one in eight. As for the premise itself, I give about four chances in five that machines can do nearly all mental work by 2032, and about three in five that they can do nearly all work of every kind by 2045 (@f-premise).

== What the guide leaves out

It is about the economy of the United States, with other countries mentioned in passing. It treats the dangers of advanced AI that are not economic, such as loss of human control over the systems or their use as weapons, only where they bear on the argument. They may well matter more than anything discussed here. And it is written at one date. The facts in Part IV are those of 12 November 2026, and some will be out of date within months.
